{#
  Editorial masthead: shared frame, included by the base layout rather than
  composed per page. Content comes from _data/site.js and _data/nav.js.

  ONE COMPOSITION THAT CONDENSES INTO ITSELF, from the Header Condense comp,
  option C. The editorial rail expanded, a 62px bar condensed. Past 96px of
  scroll the four nav items part (two left, two right), the row rises, and the
  mark settles into the gap between What We Do and Results. Back above 48px it
  plays in reverse. The two thresholds are deliberately apart so it cannot
  flicker when someone rests on the boundary.

  THE TWO STATES ARE CHOSEN INDEPENDENTLY. The expanded rail is the original
  composition, untouched, sized by its own content. The condensed bar is its own
  62px arrangement. The travel between them is measured at runtime, never
  written down. Deriving either from the other is what regressed this once.

  This REPLACED a masthead plus a separate sticky bar plus a sentinel. Above
  1120 there is now one header and one nav list rather than two of each, which
  is also why the "one nav per viewport" note below got simpler rather than
  harder. Below 1120 the sticky bar survives as the mobile bar, unchanged.

  Everything that moves is a transform. Only the header's own height animates,
  and a fixed-height spacer holds the page still so nothing below it shifts.

  data-masthead-nav IS LOAD-BEARING. main.js measures the four labels through it
  to derive the condensed offsets. The comp's own version queried an attribute
  that did not exist in its markup, so its measurement never ran and it silently
  fell back to a symmetric split. If this attribute is dropped, the same happens
  here and nothing errors.

  The wordmark A carries the crossbar-less alt-glyph, scoped to that single
  character. Never word-level font-feature-settings: ss01 also carries D, E, H,
  J, O and R, which the lockup has retired. The isotype is alt="" because the
  adjacent text already names the link.

  Below 1120px the composition does not survive and is replaced rather than
  squeezed: four items plus a mark cannot be centred in a 390px bar without the
  items becoming unreadable, and a logo descending into a row that does not
  exist is a logo that just moves. THE STICKY BAR IS THAT MOBILE BAR, always
  present and compacting on scroll rather than appearing. The four nav items
  move into the ink menu pane at the foot of this file.

  ONE nav list per viewport is exposed to assistive tech: above 1120 this
  header's, below it only the menu's. The hidden ones are display:none.

  The location line goes on condense and the booking line stays. Booking is the
  only call to action in the header and the condensed bar is the state a reader
  spends most time in, so removing it would take the CTA away exactly when it is
  most likely to be wanted. It DOES travel, unlike in the comp: the comp parked
  it at the top edge where it was already inside the condensed bar, but this
  header centres it against the lockup at 71.7px, which is outside a 62px bar.
  So it gets the same treatment as everything else, a measured translate between
  its two chosen positions.
#}
{% from "components/icon.njk" import icon %}

{#- THE EXPANDED STATE IS THE ORIGINAL COMPOSITION, UNCHANGED. Three-column
    rail with the labels centred against the full lockup, then the nav in its
    own band below a rule with real space above and below it. Nothing about the
    condense reaches back into it: the two states are chosen independently and
    the travel between them is MEASURED. See masthead.css. -#}
<header class="cs-masthead" data-masthead data-cond="false">
  <div class="cs-masthead__rail">
    <p class="cs-masthead__label cs-masthead__location">{{ site.location }}</p>

    {#- THE ORIENTATION SLOT. Empty in the markup; main.js fills it with the
        name of the section under the bar, and only on a page that declares
        sections with data-orient. Pages that declare none never light it and
        keep exactly the behaviour they had.

        IT SHARES THE LOCATION'S GRID CELL rather than taking a fourth column.
        The rail is 1fr auto 1fr and the lockup has to stay centred on the
        middle track, so both labels are placed in column 1 row 1 and cross-fade
        in place. That is what makes this the same position rather than a
        position near it.

        aria-hidden, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ACCESSIBILITY DESIGN. This duplicates
        the heading the reader is already inside, and its content changes on
        scroll. Exposed, it would either be announced on every threshold
        crossing or sit in the tree going stale. The location beside it is
        untouched and still read. Nothing is added to or removed from the
        accessibility tree while scrolling: both elements are always present and
        only their opacity changes. -#}
    <p class="cs-masthead__label cs-masthead__orient" aria-hidden="true" data-orient-slot></p>

    {#- Two spans around the icon and nothing else. The descent and the shrink
        are two transitions on two elements, so the mark starts travelling
        before it begins to scale; without that offset the two resolve together
        and read as a zoom-out rather than a move. The lockup is otherwise
        untouched: .cs-wordmark--stacked still owns every size in it. -#}
    <a class="cs-wordmark cs-wordmark--stacked" href="/" aria-label="{{ site.name }}, home">
      <span class="cs-masthead__markpos"><span class="cs-masthead__markscale">
        <img class="cs-wordmark__icon" src="/brand/Circulation-Studio-icon.svg" alt="" width="117" height="117">
      </span></span>
      <span class="cs-wordmark__name">Circul<span class="alt-soft">a</span>tion</span>
      <span class="cs-wordmark__studio">Studio</span>
    </a>

    <a class="cs-masthead__label cs-masthead__booking" href="{{ site.booking.url }}">
      {{ site.booking.label }}
    </a>
  </div>

  <nav class="cs-masthead__nav" aria-label="Primary">
    <ul class="cs-masthead__navlist" data-masthead-nav>
      {% for item in nav.primary %}
        <li><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </nav>
</header>

{# Fixed height, and it never changes. The header is fixed and condenses over
   the top of it, so the page below does not move at all while it plays. Below
   1120 it becomes the mobile bar's spacer instead. #}
<div class="cs-masthead__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<div class="cs-stickybar" data-sticky-masthead>
  <a class="cs-wordmark cs-wordmark--inline" href="/" aria-label="{{ site.name }}, home">
    <img class="cs-wordmark__icon" src="/brand/Circulation-Studio-icon.svg" alt="" width="117" height="117">
    <span class="cs-wordmark__name">Circul<span class="alt-soft">a</span>tion Studio</span>

    {#- THE MOBILE SLOT. Past the threshold this replaces the wordmark's TEXT,
        not the link. The isotype stays, the href stays, and the anchor's
        aria-label still reads "Circulation Studio, home", so the accessible
        name of this link never changes and home is still one tap away. A screen
        reader sees the same link before and after; only the printed word
        changes, and that word is aria-hidden. -#}
    <span class="cs-stickybar__orient" aria-hidden="true" data-orient-slot></span>
  </a>

  <nav class="cs-stickybar__nav" aria-label="Primary, condensed">
    {% for item in nav.primary %}
      <a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
    {% endfor %}
  </nav>

  {# Mobile and tablet controls, hidden above 1120px where the nav above
     carries the same destinations inline. The booking note shows on the
     tablet only; on the phone it moves into the menu foot with the location,
     per the design. #}
  <div class="cs-stickybar__controls">
    <a class="cs-stickybar__booking" href="{{ site.booking.url }}">{{ site.booking.label }}</a>

    <button class="cs-stickybar__toggle" type="button"
            data-menu-toggle aria-controls="cs-menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Open menu">
      {{ icon("menu", 24) }}
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

{# Mobile menu: full-pane ink overlay. Rendered at every width and hidden
   above 1120px in CSS, so there is one copy of it and no template branch.

   Open and close state, the focus trap and Escape live in main.js. The pane is
   hidden with visibility, not the hidden attribute, which keeps it out of the
   tab order while closed and still lets the fade run.

   ALT-GLYPH: the single A here, nothing else. Not the nav labels, not the
   location, not the booking note. The wordmark carries no icon on this pane,
   but that does NOT license the O device: DESIGN_SYSTEM.md permits the O only
   where the mark is absent AND the type is not the wordmark. The wordmark
   never carries a device on its O, in any lockup. #}
<div class="cs-menu" id="cs-menu" data-menu>
  <div class="cs-menu__bar">
    {# Inert on purpose, matching the design. The closed bar's lockup is the
       link home; a second one here would put a fifth destination in a
       four-item menu. #}
    <span class="cs-wordmark cs-wordmark--inline cs-wordmark--on-deep">
      <span class="cs-wordmark__name">Circul<span class="alt-soft">a</span>tion Studio</span>
    </span>

    <button class="cs-menu__dismiss" type="button" data-menu-close aria-label="Close menu">
      {{ icon("close", 24) }}
    </button>
  </div>

  <nav class="cs-menu__nav" aria-label="Primary">
    {% for item in nav.primary %}
      <a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
    {% endfor %}
  </nav>

  <div class="cs-menu__foot">
    <span class="cs-menu__location">{{ site.location }}</span>
    <a class="cs-menu__booking" href="{{ site.booking.url }}">{{ site.booking.label }}</a>
  </div>
</div>
